## How to Improve This Paper

### Missing Experiments (top 3-5 only)
1. **Direct comparison to prior hyperbolic DT/RF methods (Chlenski et al., 2024; Doorenbos et al., 2023) on hyperbolic benchmarks.** The paper claims to generalize these methods, yet never benchmarks against them. Without this, it is impossible to know whether the angular reformulation preserves, improves, or degrades performance on the single-curvature setting it subsumes.

2. **Ablation: product DT vs. ensemble of per-component DTs.** A core claim is that joint splits across components provide value (Section 3.4). Without comparing to independently trained trees on each component manifold, there is no evidence that the product structure is doing anything useful beyond having access to all features.

3. **Evaluation on higher-dimensional manifolds.** All benchmarks use D=2 or D=4 component manifolds. The method claims generality to arbitrary dimensionality; without testing at higher D, it is unclear whether the O(1) angular splits remain competitive versus baselines that can exploit richer feature spaces.

4. **Product-space perceptron evaluated on product manifold benchmarks.** It is omitted from single-curvature results as non-competitive, but it is the most directly comparable existing method for product manifolds (Tabaghi et al., 2021). Its absence from Table 2 undermines the product-manifold claims.

### Deeper Analysis Needed (top 3-5 only)
1. **Justification for the "maximum-margin" claim.** The abstract and contributions state splits are maximum-margin, but no proof, derivation, or empirical margin analysis is provided. This is a strong theoretical claim left entirely unsupported.

2. **Analysis of when and why the method loses to k-NN on product manifolds.** k-NN outperforms Product DTs/RFs on 7/8 synthetic multi-K classification benchmarks and on most link prediction tasks (Table 2). The paper celebrates "top-2" rankings but never investigates why the proposed method is not top-1 on its core use case (product manifolds).

3. **Reporting max(DT, RF) obscures individual model performance.** Tables 2–3 aggregate by taking the better of DT and RF per method, making it impossible to assess whether the gains come from the tree algorithm or the forest ensemble. Separate DT and RF columns are needed.

4. **Sensitivity to signature selection.** The paper acknowledges signature selection is challenging, but provides no analysis of how performance changes when the wrong signature is chosen or how much the product manifold benefit depends on getting the signature right.

### Visualizations & Case Studies
1. **Decision boundary visualizations on hyperbolic and product manifolds.** Only the S² landmasses visualization exists (Figure 5). Visualizations on H² and H²×S² are essential to verify that the claimed geodesically convex splits actually produce meaningful boundaries in non-spherical geometries.

2. **Failure case analysis on product manifold data.** When k-NN beats the product DT/RF (e.g., all synthetic multi-K classification rows), showing what the decision boundaries look like would reveal whether the angular split formulation is too restrictive for mixed-curvature data.

3. **Learned tree structure visualization.** The paper positions DTs as interpretable compared to neural networks, but never shows an actual learned tree to demonstrate this interpretability in practice.

### Obvious Next Steps
1. **Run head-to-head comparison with Chlenski et al. (2024) on their hyperbolic benchmarks.** This is the most natural baseline and its absence is conspicuous given the explicit claim of generalizing their work.

2. **Separate DT and RF results in all tables** so readers can assess the contribution of the tree algorithm independently of the ensemble.

3. **Add a product-vs-per-component ablation** to justify the central design choice of operating on the product manifold jointly rather than decomposing.

### Other stuff you can add
1. The Euclidean DT equivalence claim (Appendix C) should be verified empirically—showing identical performance to sklearn on Euclidean benchmarks would strengthen the theoretical claim.
2. Runtime comparison on the same hardware for all methods would help practitioners assess practical tradeoffs, especially since the angular formulation adds preprocessing overhead.
